# HMD-0033: `hmd-lsp` — the language server, written in Rust

**Status**: drafted
**Created**: 2026-11-25
**Source**: [the board card that opened the work](../../issues/0117-a-language-server-in-rust.hmd)

## Abstract

HyperMarkDown gets a language server, and the server is a Rust program named
`hmd-lsp`, in its own directory `tools/hmd-lsp/`. It speaks the Language
Server Protocol over standard input and output. It resolves every reference
through the `hypermarkdown` crate in `tools/hmd-rs-core/` and keeps no
resolution code of its own. It answers semantic tokens, go to definition,
hover, and diagnostics for the three ways a reference can fail to resolve;
completion and rename follow. It reads the text an editor holds in front of
the text on disk. The VS Code extension becomes a client of it, and keeps
working when the program is absent. The server ships as one native binary per
platform. This record supersedes the earlier decision that the language server
MUST be written in Python against `pygls` and live in `tools/hmd/`. Python
stays the canonical implementation and the only complete linter.

## Motivation

The work started from a question about syntax highlighting. The VS Code
extension colours a card with a TextMate grammar, and the site colours a code
sample with a Pygments lexer. Both match text with regular expressions. Neither
can know whether a link resolves, and neither serves an editor other than VS
Code. Semantic tokens from a language server do both: one implementation
colours a card in every editor that speaks the protocol, and it colours a
broken link differently from a working one.

A language server was already decided, in Python. That decision is reversed
here, for these reasons.

- **The reason for Python no longer holds.** The earlier record chose Python
  because a server written in any other language would be a third place where
  resolution behaviour lives, beside the Python and the TypeScript
  implementations. That third place now exists for another reason. HQL, the
  query language in a sibling repository, is written in Rust and needed the
  resolver as a library, so the repository gained the `hypermarkdown` crate.
  The crate produces the specified resolutions on every case of the
  conformance corpus, and its list of known differences is empty. A Rust
  server adds no implementation. It adds a caller to one that exists.
- **The Rust crate already reads text.** A language server answers about a
  buffer that may never have been saved. The crate loads a vault through a
  host trait, so a caller can place documents it holds in memory in front of
  the files. The Python implementation cannot do this yet: its workspace
  index reads every card from disk, and two changes to that index were the
  recorded blockers of the Python server.
- **A Rust server is one native binary.** The project already ships two
  native binaries to editor users, `d2` and `hql`, through a pinned toolchain
  that the extension bundles. A Rust server ships the same way. A Python
  server needs an interpreter on the user's machine, or a frozen bundle of an
  interpreter and five packages for each platform.
- **An editor starts the server with every window.** A native program starts
  in milliseconds. An interpreter that imports a Markdown library, a YAML
  library and a protocol library does not.

**Risk this record must not defer.** With this decision an editor shows the
answers of two implementations side by side: the preview and the live
diagnostics come from the TypeScript implementation, and the colours, the
hover and the definitions come from the Rust one. A disagreement between them
is visible to a user as a link coloured as broken that the preview follows.
The conformance corpus is what prevents this, and it holds both
implementations to the same expected resolutions. The specification below
therefore forbids the server from resolving anything itself.

## Goals

- A language server that any editor speaking the protocol can use, with no
  interpreter and no runtime.
- Colours that say whether a reference resolves.
- Answers about the text in the editor, saved or not.
- A VS Code extension that uses the server when it is present and loses
  nothing it has today when it is absent.

## Non-goals

- **Linting.** The server reports the three outcomes the resolver has for a
  reference that does not resolve. It does not report a missing heading, a
  repeated block anchor, a malformed import, or any other rule of the linter.
  `hmd lint` in Python stays the linter for the command line, for continuous
  integration and for the site build, and the TypeScript implementation stays
  the source of live diagnostics in VS Code.
- **Rendering.** The preview is the extension's, on the TypeScript
  implementation. The server produces no HTML and expands no embed.
- **Moving canonicity.** Python stays the canonical implementation. Where two
  implementations disagree about a case the corpus covers, Python defines the
  correct answer.
- **Replacing the TextMate grammar or the Pygments lexer.** The grammar
  colours a card before the server answers and when there is no server. The
  lexer colours code samples on the site, where there is no editor.
- **A transport other than standard input and output.**

## Specification

### The tool

- The server MUST be a Rust program in `tools/hmd-lsp/`, built as one binary
  named `hmd-lsp`. The Cargo package is named `hypermarkdown-lsp`, because
  the name `hmd-lsp` on crates.io belongs to an unrelated project. It is its own Cargo package with its own committed
  `Cargo.lock`, outside both the uv workspace and the npm workspace, as the
  resolver crate is.
- The package MUST depend on the `hypermarkdown` crate by path, at
  `../hmd-rs-core`. A change to the resolver then reaches the server on the
  next build, and the two cannot drift apart inside one commit.
- The package MUST NOT be folded into the resolver crate. HQL depends on that
  crate as a library, and a protocol library has no place among a resolver's
  dependencies.
- The tool owns its `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `LICENSE` and `DEVELOP.md`,
  as every tool does. Its work is tracked in `doc/status/lsp.hmd`. Its release
  tag carries the prefix `lsp-v`.

### Resolution is the crate's

- The server MUST obtain every resolution from the `hypermarkdown` crate. It
  MUST NOT contain a rule that decides which card a reference names.
- When an answer needs something the crate does not offer, the crate gains it
  and the server calls it. The first case of this is loading a vault from disk
  through a host the caller supplies, which the crate gains with this record.
- The server MAY read the text of a reference to find where its parts lie,
  because where a page name starts and ends is presentation and decides
  nothing.

### The transport and the program

- The server MUST speak the Language Server Protocol, as JSON-RPC messages
  with `Content-Length` framing, over standard input and standard output. It
  MUST write nothing else to standard output.
- `hmd-lsp --stdio` and `hmd-lsp` with no argument both start the server.
  `--version` prints the name and the version, and `--help` prints the usage.
  Any other argument is an error with exit status 2.
- The server exits with status 0 after the client sends `shutdown` and then
  `exit`.
- A request the server does not answer MUST get the protocol's
  method-not-found error. A request whose parameters the server cannot read
  MUST get the invalid-parameters error. Neither ends the server.

### Documents and vaults

- The server MUST answer about the text the client last sent for a document,
  and never about the file of the same path on disk. It asks the client for
  the whole text on every change.
- A document belongs to the vault whose root is the nearest directory at or
  above the document that holds a `.hmd` directory. The namespace root is the
  directory that the vault's `location_path` setting names.
- The server MUST load a vault from disk with every open document placed in
  front of the file of the same path. A document that is open and saved
  nowhere is a card of the vault, at the path its URI names.
- A document outside every vault, a document outside its vault's namespace
  root, a document of a vault whose configuration cannot be read, and a
  document whose URI is not a `file:` URI all resolve nothing. The server
  still returns their semantic tokens, with no resolution modifier, and
  reports no diagnostic for them.
- The server MUST NOT resolve a reference across a vault boundary.
- When a document changes, opens or closes, and when the client reports a
  changed file, the server drops every loaded vault and loads again on the
  next request. This is correct and not fast; a faster path is open work.
- The server asks the client to watch `**/*.{hmd,md,markdown}` and
  `**/.hmd/config.toml` when the client allows a server to register watchers.

### Positions

- The resolver counts a column in Unicode code points, from 1. The protocol
  counts a character offset in UTF-16 code units, from 0. The server MUST
  convert every position that crosses this boundary. A card with an emoji
  before a link is the case that breaks a server that does not.
- A line ends at a line feed, at a carriage return followed by a line feed,
  and at a lone carriage return, on both sides, so a line number is the same
  number on both.

### Semantic tokens

The server answers `textDocument/semanticTokens/full`. It emits one token for
each part of each reference:

| Part of a reference | Token type |
| --- | --- |
| The page, as in `[[tokens]]`, without the whitespace around it | `class` |
| The fragment with its `#`, as in `#Rotation` or `#^rotation` | `property` |
| The display text after the first vertical bar | `string` |

Every token of one reference carries the same modifiers:

| Modifier | The reference |
| --- | --- |
| `unresolved` | names no card |
| `ambiguous` | names more than one card |
| `outside` | has a path that leaves the namespace |
| `embed` | is an embed, written `![[…]]` |

- The token types MUST be standard types of the protocol, so that a theme
  colours them with no configuration.
- The modifiers are the server's own. A client that does not know them
  ignores them, and a client that knows them gives each a colour.
- A reference inside a code span, a fenced block or a comment is not a
  reference and gets no token, because the resolver does not report it.
- The brackets get no token. The grammar in the editor colours them.

### Go to definition

- On a reference that resolves, the answer is the start of the card it names.
- On an ambiguous reference, the answer is the start of every candidate, so
  the editor offers the choice.
- On any other reference, and outside every reference, the answer is empty.
- The position just after the closing brackets counts as on the reference.

### Hover

- On a reference, the answer says what the reference resolves to: the path of
  the card and the rule that found it, or that no card has the name, or the
  list of candidates, or that the path leaves the namespace.
- The rules are named in words: the path in the reference, a named import, the
  spine, a directory imported with `*`, and autodiscovery.

### Diagnostics

The server publishes diagnostics for every open document after each change.
It reports three outcomes, with the canonical linter's code, severity and
message for each:

| Outcome | Code | Severity | Message |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| The reference names no card | `HMD001` | warning | `[[x]] does not resolve to a page` |
| The reference names several cards | `HMD002` | error | `[[x]] matches 2 pages; qualify it (candidates: a/x.hmd, b/x.hmd)` |
| The path leaves the namespace | `HMD003` | error | `[[../x]] resolves outside the namespace root` |

- The source of each diagnostic is `hmd`.
- The server publishes for every open document after any change, because a
  change to one card can change what a reference in another resolves to.
- A client MAY turn diagnostics off by sending `{"diagnostics": false}` as the
  initialization options. The server then publishes none.

### The VS Code extension is a client

- The extension MUST look for the program in this order: the path in a
  setting, the bundled toolchain directory, then `PATH`. This is the order it
  already uses for `d2` and `hql`.
- The extension MUST start the server with diagnostics turned off, because
  the TypeScript implementation already reports the same three outcomes and
  the rest of the linter's rules. A user would otherwise see each problem
  twice.
- **The extension MUST keep working without the server.** When it finds no
  program, or the program fails to start, the preview, the graph, the cells
  and the live diagnostics behave exactly as before, and the grammar colours
  the card. The extension says once that the server is absent and what it
  adds.
- The extension MUST NOT start the server in a virtual workspace, where there
  is no file system for a process to read, or in an untrusted workspace.
- The extension contributes a colour for each of the server's modifiers, so a
  broken link stands out in a theme that knows nothing of HyperMarkDown.

### Distribution

- The server is released as one binary for each platform the toolchain
  serves: Linux on x86-64 and ARM64, macOS on x86-64 and ARM64, and Windows on
  x86-64.
- A release is built by a workflow in this repository on a tag with the
  prefix `lsp-v`, and the binaries are attached to the GitHub release.
- The package is published to crates.io as `hypermarkdown-lsp`, so that
  `cargo install hypermarkdown-lsp` builds the program on a platform that has
  no archive. A published package cannot depend on a path, so the resolver
  crate is released first whenever the server needs a newer version of it.
- The pinned toolchain carries the binary beside `d2` and `hql`, and the
  extension bundles the toolchain, so a user of the extension installs
  nothing.

## Backwards Compatibility

Nothing a user has installed changes. No language server was ever released,
in any language.

The records change. The earlier decision that the server MUST be Python on
`pygls` is superseded by this record, and the two changes to the Python
workspace index that it required are no longer blockers of anything. They
stay worthwhile on their own, for any caller that wants to lint unsaved text.

## Security Considerations

- The server starts no process and opens no network connection.
- The server reads files only through the resolver's host. The containment
  rules are the resolver's: a path is normalised and checked against the
  namespace root, a symbolic link out of the root is never followed, and a
  nested vault is never entered.
- A go-to-definition answer names only a card of the vault the document
  belongs to, so the server cannot be made to point an editor at a file
  outside the namespace.
- A card is untrusted input. The resolver's parser never fails on a card, and
  the server MUST NOT end on one.
- The server holds every open document and every loaded vault in memory, and
  loads a vault again after each change. A very large vault costs time on
  every keystroke, not memory without bound.

## Deployment / Activation

1. The resolver crate gains loading from disk through a caller's host.
2. The server answers semantic tokens, definition, hover and diagnostics, and
   has a job in continuous integration.
3. The extension becomes a client, with the program found by a setting or on
   `PATH`.
4. A release workflow builds the binaries, and the toolchain carries them.
5. The extension bundles the server through the toolchain.
6. Completion and rename.

## Reference Implementation

- `tools/hmd-rs-core/src/vault.rs` — `Vault::open_with`, which loads a vault
  from disk through a host the caller builds over the namespace root.
- `tools/hmd-lsp/src/server.rs` — the capabilities and the message loop.
- `tools/hmd-lsp/src/workspace.rs` — the open documents, the loaded vaults,
  and the host that puts a document in front of its file.
- `tools/hmd-lsp/src/tokens.rs`, `features.rs`, `links.rs`, `lines.rs`,
  `uri.rs` — the tokens, the three other answers, the parts of a reference,
  the position conversion, and `file:` URIs.
- `tools/hmd-vsc-ext/` — the client, a setting for the program's path, and
  the colours of the modifiers.
- `.github/workflows/ci.yml` — a job for the server.

## Test Plan

The protocol tests run a server on a thread and speak the protocol to it, and
one test runs a session against the built program.

Unit tests MUST include:

- the position conversion, with a character outside the Basic Multilingual
  Plane, a byte-order mark, and each of the three line endings;
- `file:` URIs in their Unix and Windows forms, with a drive letter, a share,
  an escaped character, and a scheme that is not `file`;
- the parts of a reference, with and without a fragment and display text.

Protocol tests MUST include:

- the tokens of a reference that resolves, that names no card, that names two
  cards, and that leaves the namespace;
- a reference to a card that exists only as an unsaved document;
- a document outside every vault, and a vault with a broken configuration;
- definition on a resolved and on an ambiguous reference;
- the three diagnostics with their codes, severities and messages, and none
  when the client turns them off;
- a request the server does not know, and a request with broken parameters.

```bash
cd tools/hmd-lsp
cargo fmt --check
cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings
cargo test --locked
```

## Open Questions

- Does go to definition on a reference with a fragment lead to the heading or
  the block, and where does the code that finds a heading live? The resolver
  crate reads no heading today, and how a heading's text becomes the name a
  fragment matches is a rule of the language.
- Do block anchors and frontmatter get tokens? The crate reports neither
  position today.
- Does the server report the linter's other rules to an editor other than VS
  Code, by a port of the rules to Rust or by calling `hmd lint` when it is
  installed?
- Is the toolchain repository the place that builds the server's binaries, or
  does it take them from this repository's release as it takes `hql` from
  HQL's?
- ~~Is the server published to crates.io?~~ **Answered 2026-11-25: yes, as
  `hypermarkdown-lsp`.** The name `hmd-lsp` there is another project's.
- How does the server update one card of a loaded vault without loading the
  vault again?

